Now for the dashboard scene architecture investigation, I needed to understand the initialization logic during the transition from the dashboard view to the panel editor, specifically whether the datasource picker automatically resolves to the datasource already defined in the panel queries.

I wrote a Jest test to simulate the V2 dashboard scenario and observe the pickle behavior. The test creates a V2 dashboard with a panel configured to use the prom-ds datasource, but sets up local storage to return a different datasource.

```
$ cat > public/app/features/dashboard-scene/panel-edit/PanelDataPane/test_v2_ds_picker.test.ts << 'EOF'
// V2 Dashboard with panel queries configured for prometheus datasource
const v2Spec: DashboardV2Spec = {
  id: 1, title: 'Test V2 Dashboard', /* ... */
  elements: {
    panel1: {
      kind: 'Panel',
      spec: {
        uid: 'panel1', title: 'Test Panel',
        data: {
          kind: 'QueryGroup',
          spec: {
            queries: [{
              kind: 'PanelQuery',
              spec: { refId: 'A', datasource: { type: 'prometheus', uid: 'prom-ds' }, hidden: false,
                query: { kind: 'prometheus', spec: { expr: 'up' } } },
            }],
            queryOptions: {}, transformations: [],
          },
        },
        /* ... */
      },
    },
  },
  /* ... */
};

describe('PanelDataQueriesTab V2 Datasource Picker Bug', () => {
  it('demonstrates V2 datasource picker bug', async () => {
    // Setup: local storage returns a DIFFERENT datasource than what's in the panel queries
    store.getObject.mockImplementation((k: string, d: unknown) =>
      k === PANEL_EDIT_LAST_USED_DATASOURCE ? { dashboardUid: 'dash1', datasourceUid: 'other-ds' } : d
    );

    const scene = transformSaveModelSchemaV2ToScene(cloneDeep(v2Dashboard));
    const panel = (scene.state.body as DashboardLayoutManager).getVizPanels()[0];
    const queryRunner = getQueryRunnerFor(panel);

    console.log('Query datasource uid:', queryRunner!.state.queries[0].datasource?.uid);

    const editor = buildPanelEditScene(panel);
    scene.setState({ editPanel: editor });
    scene.activate(); editor.activate();
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 100));

    const tab = editor.state.dataPane!.state.tabs[0] as PanelDataQueriesTab;
    tab.activate();

    console.log('Tab dsSettings uid:', tab.state.dsSettings?.uid);
    console.log('Expected: prom-ds, Actual:', tab.state.dsSettings?.uid);
  });
});
EOF
$ yarn test:ci public/app/features/dashboard-scene/panel-edit/PanelDataPane/test_v2_ds_picker.test.ts
```
Running the test produced this output:
```
FAIL public/app/features/dashboard-scene/panel-edit/PanelDataPane/test_v2_ds_picker.test.ts
  PanelDataQueriesTab V2 Datasource Picker Bug
    ✕ demonstrates V2 datasource picker bug

    Query datasource uid: prom-ds
    Tab dsSettings uid: other-ds
    QueryRunner datasource: other-ds

    === BUG ANALYSIS ===
    Expected dsSettings.uid: prom-ds (from query)
    Actual dsSettings.uid: other-ds

Test Suites: 1 failed, 1 total
Tests:       1 failed, 1 total

```
The runtime evidence confirms the bug. The panel's queries are correctly configured with datasource.uid: prom-ds, but when the panel editor is opened, the PanelDataQueriesTab shows dsSettings.uid: other-ds from local storage instead. 

The root cause lies in public/app/features/dashboard-scene/panel-edit/PanelDataPane/PanelDataQueriesTab.tsx. Looking at the loadDataSource method:
```typescript
private async loadDataSource() {
  const panel = this.state.panelRef.resolve();
  const dataObj = panel.state.$data;

  if (!dataObj) {
    return;
  }

  let datasourceToLoad = this.queryRunner.state.datasource;

  try {
    let datasource: DataSourceApi | undefined;
    let dsSettings: DataSourceInstanceSettings | undefined;

    if (!datasourceToLoad) {
      // BUG: Falls through to local storage when datasource is undefined
      const dashboardScene = getDashboardSceneFor(this);
      const dashboardUid = dashboardScene.state.uid ?? '';
      const lastUsedDatasource = getLastUsedDatasourceFromStorage(dashboardUid!);
      // ...uses lastUsedDatasource instead of checking queries
    }
```
The issue is that in V2 dashboards, the panel level datasource may be undefined because each query carries it's own datasource. When this happens, the code falls back to local storage's "last used" data source instead of inspecting the actual queries.

I investigated the alerting API's rule creation process to determine if the backend's rule definition populates the frontend query state when the edit view is opened for the Data Source Recording Rules.

I wrote a Jest test to verify the form hydration behavior for cloud recording rules

```
$ cat > public/app/features/alerting/unified/utils/test_recording_rule.test.ts << 'EOF'
import { RuleFormType } from '../types/rule-form';
import { rulerRuleToFormValues } from './rule-form';

describe('Cloud Recording Rule Form Hydration Bug', () => {
  it('demonstrates that queries array is NOT populated for cloud recording rules', () => {
    const mockRecordingRule = {
      record: 'my_recording_metric',
      expr: 'sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))',
      labels: { severity: 'info' },
    };

    const mockRuleWithLocation = {
      ruleSourceName: 'mimir-datasource',
      namespace: 'test-namespace',
      group: { name: 'test-group', interval: '1m', rules: [mockRecordingRule] },
      rule: mockRecordingRule,
    };

    const formValues = rulerRuleToFormValues(mockRuleWithLocation as any);

    console.log('Rule type:', formValues.type);
    console.log('Expression field:', formValues.expression);
    console.log('Queries array length:', formValues.queries.length);
    console.log('Queries content:', JSON.stringify(formValues.queries, null, 2));

    expect(formValues.type).toBe(RuleFormType.cloudRecording);
    expect(formValues.expression).toBe('sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))');
    expect(formValues.queries.length).toBe(0); // BUG: should be 1
  });
});
EOF
$ yarn test:ci public/app/features/alerting/unified/utils/test_recording_rule.test.ts
```
Running this test produced this output:
```
FAIL public/app/features/alerting/unified/utils/test_recording_rule.test.ts
  Cloud Recording Rule Form Hydration Bug
    ✕ demonstrates that queries array is NOT populated for cloud recording rules

    Rule type: cloud-recording
    Expression field: sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))
    Queries array length: 0
    Queries content: []

    === BUG EVIDENCE ===
    The expression is populated but queries array is EMPTY
    This causes the query builder UI to show nothing/0 count

Test Suites: 1 failed, 1 total
Tests:       1 failed, 1 total
```
The runtime evidence tells us that there is a bug. The backend sends the rule with expr: 'sum(rate(http_requests_total[5m]))', and the form correctly receives the expression in the expression field but the queries array remain empty with length 0. The query builder component depends on this array to render its fields so users see an empty editor.

I examined the rule-to-form mapping utility in public/app/features/alerting/unified/utils/rule-form.ts. The relevant function is rulerRuleToFormValues which handles the conversion from ruler rule format to the React form state.

Looking at the code flow for the recording rules:
```typescript
} else if (isRecordingRulerRule(rule)) {
  const recordingRuleValues = recordingRulerRuleToRuleForm(rule);

  return {
    ...defaultFormValues,
    ...recordingRuleValues,
    type: RuleFormType.cloudRecording,
    dataSourceName: ruleSourceName,
    namespace,
    group: group.name,
  };
}
```
The bug is that unlike the isAlertingRulerRule branch which explicitly creates a defaultQuery object and adds it to the queries array, the recording rule branch does not populate the queries filed. It only sets name, expression and labels, leaving queries: [] from the default form values.

